 "_note": "The 2-D TRANSFORM plane. THE LAW: a matrix is the six-number affine [a,b,c,d,e,f] \u2014 x' = a\u00b7x + c\u00b7y + e, y' = b\u00b7x + d\u00b7y + f \u2014 the SVG and Canvas2D spelling, NOT a mat3 and NOT the scene kernel's column-major mat4 (a 2-D canvas has no third axis and borrowing the 3-D shape would invite silent index drift). The `transform` attribute takes the SVG function list \u2014 translate \u00b7 scale \u00b7 rotate (optionally about a centre) \u00b7 skewX \u00b7 skewY \u00b7 matrix, degrees \u2014 and applies it LEFT TO RIGHT: 'translate(10,0) scale(2)' means translate \u2218 scale, the leftmost function outermost, so M = M1\u00b7M2\u00b7\u2026\u00b7Mn. Composition down the tree is world = parentWorld \u00b7 local, the same fold the scene kernel uses one dimension up. ARTICLE 7: an unknown function, a wrong-arity function, a non-numeric argument or junk between functions costs exactly ONE diagnostic and is SKIPPED \u2014 every other function in the list still applies. `parse` pins the matrix and mapped points; `compose` pins the world matrix of each display-list op in a nested tree. Expected values come from an independent scratch implementation.",
